  "tldr": "Known natural-value interventions that translate a node's complete parent vector reveal continuous-version differences of its structural mechanism while cancelling an arbitrary parent-dependent disturbance regression. The exact theorem identifies every mechanism, the complete joint residual law, and every recursively support-closed hard-intervention law if and only if the translation-difference operator has only constants in its kernel. The recovery map is single-valued only under that cover; otherwise a set-valued inverse remains, and a four-point Rademacher construction supplies bounded non-Gaussian observational-and-regime equivalence with target disagreement. A fully specified Wasserstein local experiment isolates stable budgeted design as the open question.",
  "project_justification": {
    "gap": "Published confounded additive-noise cover results use surgical parent interventions and Gaussian disturbances, while graphical soft-intervention and linear environment-shift results do not recover nonlinear mechanisms or a dependent residual law from natural-value translations.",
    "niche": "Natural-value shift experiments preserve baseline heterogeneity and create observable within-mechanism increments even when latent confounding biases every observational regression.",
    "fill": "The translation-difference null space supplies an exact necessary-and-sufficient design criterion, a support-closed residual-pushforward functional, a matching bounded non-Gaussian witness, and a metric local experiment for the unresolved stability problem."
  },
  "related_work": "Elahi, Ghasemi, and Kocaoglu establish a necessary-and-sufficient hard-intervention cover for average outcomes under multivariate Gaussian disturbances; their theorem informs the cover question, whereas the present target is a nonlinear natural-value-shift operator and complete postintervention laws. Correa and Bareinboim develop the \\(\\sigma\\)-calculus and completeness theory for stochastic interventions and soft transportability in unrestricted graphical models; those results inform the intervention semantics but do not recover an additive mechanism or joint residual distribution. Sani, Lee, and Shpitser distinguish shifts of natural treatment values from recursively defined shift policies and give graphical identification and estimation results; their natural-value semantics is the one used here. Kocaoglu, Jaber, Shanmugam, and Bareinboim characterize latent-variable graph equivalence under soft environments, targeting structure recovery rather than full-law effects. BackShift uses changes in exogenous noise distributions across environments and second-moment joint diagonalization to recover linear, possibly cyclic structure under unknown shift interventions. Causal Dantzig likewise uses additive environment interventions and covariance invariance for inference on linear structural coefficients with hidden variables. In contrast, the present interventions shift observed natural parent values by known amounts, the mechanisms may be nonlinear on a known acyclic graph, and the target is mechanism plus full intervention-law recovery rather than graph or coefficient recovery. Peters, Mooij, Janzing, and Schölkopf identify additive-noise graphs under independent disturbances; the independent-noise proposition records the corresponding no-confounding reduction. Jeunen, Gilligan-Lee, Mehrotra, and Lalmas provide the International Stroke Trial semi-synthetic intervention-disentanglement benchmark, which is an empirical stress test for residual-law recovery and not evidence for a new clinical aspirin effect.",
  "technical_internal_limitation": "The natural-value shift law is a cross-world intervention preserving each unit's baseline shifted coordinate. An environment that only changes a parent marginal or its exogenous noise distribution need not satisfy the contrast identity. This design-semantic distinction separates the kernel from BackShift and Causal Dantzig and is not a conclusion about arbitrary soft interventions.",
  "honest_scope": "Population exact identification is claimed for a known DAG, continuous bounded mechanisms on possibly unbounded closed domains, continuous conditional-mean versions, exactly implemented parent-complete natural-value shifts, and hard targets in \\(\\mathcal A_K(M)\\). Membership in \\(\\mathcal A_K(M)\\) requires every recursively generated parent vector to stay in its \\(K_i\\) almost surely and its reached support to be contained in \\(K_i\\), so unbounded residual support never licenses an undefined structural evaluation. The theorem does not claim graph recovery, a clinical aspirin conclusion, or finite-sample inference. The Wasserstein local minimax rate and optimal stable design remain open. Under mutually independent disturbances and targets confined to observational parent supports, the functional reduces to ordinary observational additive-noise residual recovery.",
  "seeds": [
    "seed:full-law-iff-cover",
